  </p>

  <h2 id="bioinformatics">Bioinformatics and computational biology: the most direct fit</h2>

  <p>
    This is where PCB with Computer Science lines up most cleanly with an actual field.
  </p>

  <p>
    Bioinformatics takes biological data — DNA sequences, protein structures, genomics datasets — and uses code to analyse, visualise, and interpret it. Computational biology goes further, building mathematical models of biological processes.
  </p>

  <ul>
    {bioinformaticsFacts.map((item) => (
      <li>{item}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A bioinformatics bachelor's degree alone is a foundation, not a finish line. The field in India is still developing, and the strongest outcomes usually come from a master's degree, a certification like BINC, or a genuinely visible project portfolio — not the undergraduate label by itself.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="health-informatics">Health informatics and healthcare IT: real, but a second-degree route</h2>

  <p>
    Health informatics blends healthcare knowledge with information systems and technology.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is a real and growing field in India, driven partly by the Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission pushing hospitals to digitise records and systems.
  </p>

  <ul>
    {healthInformaticsFacts.map((item) => (
      <li>{item}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <p class="note-line">
    Treat this as a path you reach after a health-science bachelor's degree, not a direct route straight out of 12th. Plan the longer runway honestly if this is the one that excites you most.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data-healthcare">Data and analytics roles in healthcare or pharma: the lowest-gatekeeping path</h2>

  <p>
    This is the bucket with the fewest formal degree requirements and the highest reward for demonstrated skill.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pharma and healthcare employers are actively hiring for AI- and data-linked roles in drug discovery, genomics, and clinical trials.
  </p>

  <ul>
    {dataHealthcareFacts.map((item) => (
      <li>{item}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <p class="note-line">
    <strong>Building a high-income skill portfolio matters more here than any single course name.</strong> A PCB-with-CS student who pairs a life-science degree with real Python, SQL, and statistics proof of work usually out-competes a peer who only holds a matching-sounding certificate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="biomedical-engineering">Biomedical engineering: the honest gate</h2>

  <p>
    This is the path where expectations most often collide with reality.
  </p>

  <p>
    Biomedical engineering designs and maintains medical devices and equipment — a genuinely interesting field for students who like biology and machines together.
  </p>

  <p>
    But the mainstream route into it, through JEE Main and JEE Advanced, requires Mathematics as a compulsory Class 12 subject. A PCB-with-Computer-Science student without Maths cannot sit JEE on that basis.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Still open to you</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Some state-level or institute-specific entrance exams and private colleges run their own biomedical engineering admission routes that may accept PCB without Maths — verify each one individually.</li>
        <li>Bridge-course options exist at some private universities, similar to how some BCA programmes handle a Maths gap.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Realistically closed for now</div>
      <ul>
        <li>JEE Main and JEE Advanced-based admission to IITs, NITs, and most top government biomedical engineering programmes.</li>
        <li>Any biomedical engineering route that lists Mathematics as a compulsory Class 12 subject without exception.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    If biomedical engineering is genuinely the goal, the honest next step is checking whether adding Mathematics is realistic for you, not assuming the Computer Science subject already covers it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tech-pivot">Pivoting fully into tech: BCA and what it does and does not give you</h2>

  <p>
    Some PCB-with-CS students realise, partway through, that biology was never the real pull. Computer Science was.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is a legitimate discovery, not a failure.
  </p>

  <p>
    BCA is the most direct no-Maths tech route. Most private universities accept it without Mathematics as a strict requirement, since it is a computer-applications degree rather than an engineering one. Government and central institutions are more likely to insist on it, so this varies by college and needs individual verification.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      BCA is real and respectable, but it is not identical to a Maths-based Computer Science degree in every hiring context. Some technically demanding roles and some employers still lean toward Maths-heavy CS graduates.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gap is closeable with strong, visible proof of work — projects, internships, and demonstrated skill matter more here than the degree name alone.
    </p>
  </div>

